From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-gpu: enforce max_hostmem on the virgl resource path

The virtio-gpu device has a max_hostmem property (256 MiB by default)
that is supposed to bound how much host memory a guest can make the
device allocate. virtio_gpu_resource_create_2d() honours it: it computes
the image size, refuses to allocate past the budget, and answers
VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY.

None of that happens once the guest talks GL. virtio_gpu_virgl_process_cmd()
routes RESOURCE_CREATE_2D and RESOURCE_CREATE_3D to
virgl_cmd_create_resource_2d()/_3d(), which fill in a
virgl_renderer_resource_create_args and call
virgl_renderer_resource_create() with no size computed, no budget
consulted and - worse - no check of the return value. max_hostmem is
dead code for every guest that binds a GL context, which is every
modern desktop guest.

What that costs in practice: a GNOME guest on an integrated GPU walked
host GPU memory up to roughly 950 MiB of i915 GEM objects in the
"system" region. Those are shmem-backed and charged to the memory
cgroup of the process that created them, so the cgroup OOM killer took
qemu down three times. The guest was not malicious, just a desktop.

Fix the accounting rather than the symptom:

 - Estimate a resource's host footprint from the parameters the guest
   gives us (format, width, height, depth, array_size, nr_samples,
   last_level) and charge it against the same g->hostmem counter and
   the same conf_max_hostmem limit the 2D path uses. The admission rule
   is written so it cannot overflow on a hostile request.

 - Refuse, do not reserve. Nothing is preallocated; the budget is only
   consulted at create time, and the running total reflects what has
   actually been created. When a create would go over, the resource is
   not created and the command is answered with
   VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY. A guest GL driver has to handle a
   failed resource allocation - that is an ordinary out-of-memory path
   for it. Having the host kernel kill the entire VM instead is
   strictly worse, and it takes the other domains' service with it when
   the cgroup is shared.

 - Give the memory back. virglrenderer owns virgl resources, so there is
   no g->reslist entry to hang a size off. Keep a resource_id -> bytes
   GHashTable on the VirtIOGPUGL instance, created in instance_init and
   destroyed in instance_finalize, credited on create and debited in
   virgl_cmd_resource_unref(). Without this a long-running guest that
   cycles resources normally would be starved as the counter ratcheted
   up. virtio_gpu_virgl_reset() drops the whole renderer, so the table
   and the counter are cleared there too.

 - Check virgl_renderer_resource_create()'s return value and report the
   failure to the guest instead of silently pretending the resource
   exists.

The size estimate is an estimate and the comment in the code says so.
virglrenderer hands the allocation to the GL/EGL driver, which may pad
the stride, tile the surface, promote a format or attach auxiliary
compression buffers, none of which is visible from qemu. The bits-per-
pixel table covers the VIRGL_FORMAT_* codes a desktop guest allocates in
bulk and falls back to 32 bits (4 bytes/pixel) for anything else, which
is the mode of the distribution and errs towards charging too much
rather than treating an unknown format as free. virglrenderer does not
install virgl_hw.h, so the format codes - which are fixed guest/host
protocol values - are spelled out locally rather than included.

The first refusal is logged once via qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR) so
that an operator can tell "the guest is being refused GPU memory" from
"the guest is broken", without a line per resource.

Note for operators: the 256 MiB default was never actually enforced on
the GL path, so guests that have been silently exceeding it will start
seeing refusals. A full desktop guest wants considerably more than
256 MiB; set max_hostmem to a value that fits the host's memory cgroup
budget rather than leaving the default in place.

Signed-off-by: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/tools/qemu-xen/hw/display/virtio-gpu-gl.c b/tools/qemu-xen/hw/display/virtio-gpu-gl.c
index 29d20b0132..33aee36928 100644
--- a/tools/qemu-xen/hw/display/virtio-gpu-gl.c
+++ b/tools/qemu-xen/hw/display/virtio-gpu-gl.c
@@ -104,6 +104,26 @@ static void virtio_gpu_gl_reset(VirtIODevice *vdev)
     }
 }
 
+static void virtio_gpu_gl_instance_init(Object *obj)
+{
+    VirtIOGPUGL *gl = VIRTIO_GPU_GL(obj);
+
+    /*
+     * virgl resources are owned by virglrenderer, so there is no reslist to
+     * hang the host memory accounting off. Keep a resource_id -> bytes map of
+     * our own; the value is a heap uint64_t freed by the hash table.
+     */
+    gl->virgl_hostmem = g_hash_table_new_full(g_direct_hash, g_direct_equal,
+                                              NULL, g_free);
+}
+
+static void virtio_gpu_gl_instance_finalize(Object *obj)
+{
+    VirtIOGPUGL *gl = VIRTIO_GPU_GL(obj);
+
+    g_clear_pointer(&gl->virgl_hostmem, g_hash_table_destroy);
+}
+
 static void virtio_gpu_gl_device_realize(DeviceState *qdev, Error **errp)
 {
     ERRP_GUARD();
@@ -163,6 +183,8 @@ static const TypeInfo virtio_gpu_gl_info = {
     .name = TYPE_VIRTIO_GPU_GL,
     .parent = TYPE_VIRTIO_GPU,
     .instance_size = sizeof(VirtIOGPUGL),
+    .instance_init = virtio_gpu_gl_instance_init,
+    .instance_finalize = virtio_gpu_gl_instance_finalize,
     .class_init = virtio_gpu_gl_class_init,
 };
 module_obj(TYPE_VIRTIO_GPU_GL);
diff --git a/tools/qemu-xen/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c b/tools/qemu-xen/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
index 9f34d0e661..e51352b7b0 100644
--- a/tools/qemu-xen/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
+++ b/tools/qemu-xen/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
@@ -30,6 +30,306 @@ virgl_get_egl_display(G_GNUC_UNUSED void *cookie)
 }
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * VIRGL_FORMAT_* codes used by the size estimate below. They are part of the
+ * guest/host virgl protocol and therefore fixed, but virglrenderer does not
+ * install virgl_hw.h, so the handful we care about are spelled out here
+ * instead of included.
+ */
+enum {
+    VIRGL_FMT_B8G8R8A8_UNORM        = 1,
+    VIRGL_FMT_B8G8R8X8_UNORM        = 2,
+    VIRGL_FMT_A8R8G8B8_UNORM        = 3,
+    VIRGL_FMT_X8R8G8B8_UNORM        = 4,
+    VIRGL_FMT_B5G5R5A1_UNORM        = 5,
+    VIRGL_FMT_B4G4R4A4_UNORM        = 6,
+    VIRGL_FMT_B5G6R5_UNORM          = 7,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R10G10B10A2_UNORM     = 8,
+    VIRGL_FMT_L8_UNORM              = 9,
+    VIRGL_FMT_A8_UNORM              = 10,
+    VIRGL_FMT_I8_UNORM              = 11,
+    VIRGL_FMT_L8A8_UNORM            = 12,
+    VIRGL_FMT_L16_UNORM             = 13,
+    VIRGL_FMT_UYVY                  = 14,
+    VIRGL_FMT_YUYV                  = 15,
+    VIRGL_FMT_Z16_UNORM             = 16,
+    VIRGL_FMT_Z32_UNORM             = 17,
+    VIRGL_FMT_Z32_FLOAT             = 18,
+    VIRGL_FMT_Z24_UNORM_S8_UINT     = 19,
+    VIRGL_FMT_S8_UINT_Z24_UNORM     = 20,
+    VIRGL_FMT_Z24X8_UNORM           = 21,
+    VIRGL_FMT_X8Z24_UNORM           = 22,
+    VIRGL_FMT_S8_UINT               = 23,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R32_FLOAT             = 28,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R32G32_FLOAT          = 29,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R32G32B32_FLOAT       = 30,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R32G32B32A32_FLOAT    = 31,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R16_UNORM             = 48,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R16G16_UNORM          = 49,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R16G16B16A16_UNORM    = 51,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R8_UNORM              = 64,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R8G8_UNORM            = 65,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8_UNORM          = 66,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8A8_UNORM        = 67,
+    VIRGL_FMT_X8B8G8R8_UNORM        = 68,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R16_FLOAT             = 91,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R16G16_FLOAT          = 92,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R16G16B16A16_FLOAT    = 94,
+    VIRGL_FMT_B8G8R8A8_SRGB         = 100,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8A8_SRGB         = 104,
+    VIRGL_FMT_DXT1_RGB              = 105,
+    VIRGL_FMT_DXT1_RGBA             = 106,
+    VIRGL_FMT_DXT3_RGBA             = 107,
+    VIRGL_FMT_DXT5_RGBA             = 108,
+    VIRGL_FMT_A8B8G8R8_UNORM        = 121,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R11G11B10_FLOAT       = 124,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R9G9B9E5_FLOAT        = 125,
+    VIRGL_FMT_Z32_FLOAT_S8X24_UINT  = 126,
+    VIRGL_FMT_B10G10R10A2_UNORM     = 131,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8X8_UNORM        = 134,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R8_UINT               = 177,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8A8_UINT         = 180,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R16_UINT              = 185,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R32_UINT              = 193,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R32G32B32A32_UINT     = 196,
+    VIRGL_FMT_B10G10R10X2_UNORM     = 233,
+    VIRGL_FMT_R16G16B16X16_FLOAT    = 236,
+    VIRGL_FMT_ETC2_RGB8             = 269,
+    VIRGL_FMT_ETC2_RGBA8            = 273,
+};
+
+/*
+ * Bits per pixel for the formats a desktop guest actually allocates in bulk.
+ * Anything not listed falls back to 32 bits, which is the mode of the
+ * distribution and keeps an unknown format from being accounted as free.
+ */
+static uint32_t virgl_format_bits_pp(uint32_t format)
+{
+    switch (format) {
+    case VIRGL_FMT_L8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_A8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_I8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_S8_UINT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R8_UINT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_ETC2_RGBA8:
+        return 8;
+
+    case VIRGL_FMT_B5G5R5A1_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_B4G4R4A4_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_B5G6R5_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_L8A8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_L16_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_UYVY:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_YUYV:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_Z16_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R16_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R8G8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R16_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R16_UINT:
+        return 16;
+
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8_UNORM:
+        return 24;
+
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R16G16B16A16_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R16G16B16A16_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R16G16B16X16_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R32G32_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_Z32_FLOAT_S8X24_UINT:
+        return 64;
+
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R32G32B32_FLOAT:
+        return 96;
+
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R32G32B32A32_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R32G32B32A32_UINT:
+        return 128;
+
+    /*
+     * Block compressed formats. DXT1 and ETC2_RGB8 are 8 bytes per 4x4
+     * block (4 bits/pixel); DXT3, DXT5 and ETC2_RGBA8 are 16 bytes per
+     * block (8 bits/pixel, already covered above for ETC2_RGBA8).
+     */
+    case VIRGL_FMT_DXT1_RGB:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_DXT1_RGBA:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_ETC2_RGB8:
+        return 4;
+    case VIRGL_FMT_DXT3_RGBA:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_DXT5_RGBA:
+        return 8;
+
+    /*
+     * 32 bits: every RGBA8/BGRA8 variant, the packed 10:10:10:2 and
+     * 11:11:10 formats, the 32 bit depth/stencil combinations and the
+     * single channel 32 bit formats. This is also the fallback below.
+     */
+    case VIRGL_FMT_B8G8R8A8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_B8G8R8X8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_A8R8G8B8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_X8R8G8B8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R10G10B10A2_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_Z32_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_Z32_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_Z24_UNORM_S8_UINT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_S8_UINT_Z24_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_Z24X8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_X8Z24_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R32_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R32_UINT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R16G16_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R16G16_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8A8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_X8B8G8R8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_A8B8G8R8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8X8_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8A8_UINT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_B8G8R8A8_SRGB:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R8G8B8A8_SRGB:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R11G11B10_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_R9G9B9E5_FLOAT:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_B10G10R10A2_UNORM:
+    case VIRGL_FMT_B10G10R10X2_UNORM:
+        return 32;
+
+    default:
+        /* Unrecognised: assume 4 bytes/pixel rather than assume nothing. */
+        return 32;
+    }
+}
+
+static uint64_t virgl_sat_mul(uint64_t a, uint64_t b)
+{
+    if (a != 0 && b > UINT64_MAX / a) {
+        return UINT64_MAX;
+    }
+    return a * b;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Estimate how much host memory virglrenderer will need for a resource.
+ *
+ * This is deliberately an estimate: virglrenderer hands the allocation to the
+ * GL/EGL driver, which is free to pad the stride, tile the surface, promote a
+ * format or allocate auxiliary compression buffers, and none of that is
+ * visible from here. It is close enough to keep a runaway guest inside a
+ * budget, which is all it is used for - it is not an allocator.
+ */
+static uint64_t virgl_estimate_hostmem(
+    const struct virgl_renderer_resource_create_args *args)
+{
+    uint64_t bits_pp = virgl_format_bits_pp(args->format);
+    uint64_t size;
+
+    size = virgl_sat_mul(MAX(args->width, 1), MAX(args->height, 1));
+    size = virgl_sat_mul(size, bits_pp);
+    size = size / 8 + (size % 8 ? 1 : 0);
+    size = virgl_sat_mul(size, MAX(args->depth, 1));
+    size = virgl_sat_mul(size, MAX(args->array_size, 1));
+    size = virgl_sat_mul(size, MAX(args->nr_samples, 1));
+
+    if (args->last_level) {
+        /*
+         * A full mip chain adds at most a third on top of level 0; using the
+         * limit rather than walking the levels keeps this cheap and errs on
+         * the high side, which is the safe direction for a cap.
+         */
+        size += size / 3;
+    }
+
+    return size;
+}
+
+static void virgl_hostmem_forget(VirtIOGPU *g, uint32_t resource_id)
+{
+    VirtIOGPUGL *gl = VIRTIO_GPU_GL(g);
+    gpointer val;
+
+    if (!gl->virgl_hostmem) {
+        return;
+    }
+    if (g_hash_table_lookup_extended(gl->virgl_hostmem,
+                                     GUINT_TO_POINTER(resource_id),
+                                     NULL, &val)) {
+        uint64_t size = *(uint64_t *)val;
+
+        g->hostmem = g->hostmem > size ? g->hostmem - size : 0;
+        g_hash_table_remove(gl->virgl_hostmem, GUINT_TO_POINTER(resource_id));
+    }
+}
+
+static void virgl_hostmem_remember(VirtIOGPU *g, uint32_t resource_id,
+                                   uint64_t size)
+{
+    VirtIOGPUGL *gl = VIRTIO_GPU_GL(g);
+    uint64_t *slot;
+
+    if (!gl->virgl_hostmem) {
+        return;
+    }
+    /* A re-used id should not happen, but do not leak the old charge if it does. */
+    virgl_hostmem_forget(g, resource_id);
+
+    slot = g_new(uint64_t, 1);
+    *slot = size;
+    g_hash_table_insert(gl->virgl_hostmem, GUINT_TO_POINTER(resource_id), slot);
+    g->hostmem += size;
+}
+
+static void virgl_hostmem_reset(VirtIOGPU *g)
+{
+    VirtIOGPUGL *gl = VIRTIO_GPU_GL(g);
+
+    if (gl->virgl_hostmem) {
+        g_hash_table_remove_all(gl->virgl_hostmem);
+    }
+    g->hostmem = 0;
+    gl->virgl_hostmem_capped = false;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Create a virgl resource, refusing it if it would take us over
+ * conf_max_hostmem. Returns 0 on success, with cmd->error already set
+ * otherwise.
+ */
+static int virgl_create_resource(VirtIOGPU *g,
+                                 struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *cmd,
+                                 struct virgl_renderer_resource_create_args *args)
+{
+    VirtIOGPUGL *gl = VIRTIO_GPU_GL(g);
+    uint64_t size = virgl_estimate_hostmem(args);
+    int ret;
+
+    /* Same admission rule as the 2D path, written so it cannot overflow. */
+    if (size >= g->conf_max_hostmem ||
+        g->hostmem >= g->conf_max_hostmem - size) {
+        if (!gl->virgl_hostmem_capped) {
+            gl->virgl_hostmem_capped = true;
+            qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR,
+                          "%s: virgl host memory budget exhausted: %" PRIu64
+                          " bytes charged, %" PRIu64 " more requested, "
+                          "max_hostmem=%" PRIu64 ". Refusing this and any "
+                          "further over-budget resource; raise max_hostmem if "
+                          "the guest legitimately needs more. Logged once.\n",
+                          __func__, g->hostmem, size, g->conf_max_hostmem);
+        }
+        cmd->error = VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
+        return -1;
+    }
+
+    ret = virgl_renderer_resource_create(args, NULL, 0);
+    if (ret != 0) {
+        qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR,
+                      "%s: virgl_renderer_resource_create(%u) failed: %d\n",
+                      __func__, args->handle, ret);
+        cmd->error = VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
+        return -1;
+    }
+
+    virgl_hostmem_remember(g, args->handle, size);
+    return 0;
+}
+
 static void virgl_cmd_create_resource_2d(VirtIOGPU *g,
                                          struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *cmd)
 {
@@ -51,7 +351,7 @@ static void virgl_cmd_create_resource_2d(VirtIOGPU *g,
     args.last_level = 0;
     args.nr_samples = 0;
     args.flags = VIRTIO_GPU_RESOURCE_FLAG_Y_0_TOP;
-    virgl_renderer_resource_create(&args, NULL, 0);
+    virgl_create_resource(g, cmd, &args);
 }
 
 static void virgl_cmd_create_resource_3d(VirtIOGPU *g,
@@ -75,7 +375,7 @@ static void virgl_cmd_create_resource_3d(VirtIOGPU *g,
     args.last_level = c3d.last_level;
     args.nr_samples = c3d.nr_samples;
     args.flags = c3d.flags;
-    virgl_renderer_resource_create(&args, NULL, 0);
+    virgl_create_resource(g, cmd, &args);
 }
 
 static void virgl_cmd_resource_unref(VirtIOGPU *g,
@@ -95,6 +395,7 @@ static void virgl_cmd_resource_unref(VirtIOGPU *g,
         virtio_gpu_cleanup_mapping_iov(g, res_iovs, num_iovs);
     }
     virgl_renderer_resource_unref(unref.resource_id);
+    virgl_hostmem_forget(g, unref.resource_id);
 }
 
 static void virgl_cmd_context_create(VirtIOGPU *g,
@@ -620,6 +921,8 @@ void virtio_gpu_virgl_reset_scanout(VirtIOGPU *g)
 void virtio_gpu_virgl_reset(VirtIOGPU *g)
 {
     virgl_renderer_reset();
+    /* Every virgl resource is gone with the renderer: start the budget over. */
+    virgl_hostmem_reset(g);
 }
 
 int virtio_gpu_virgl_init(VirtIOGPU *g)
diff --git a/tools/qemu-xen/include/hw/virtio/virtio-gpu.h b/tools/qemu-xen/include/hw/virtio/virtio-gpu.h
index 7a59379f5a..14323d7bae 100644
--- a/tools/qemu-xen/include/hw/virtio/virtio-gpu.h
+++ b/tools/qemu-xen/include/hw/virtio/virtio-gpu.h
@@ -231,6 +231,14 @@ struct VirtIOGPUGL {
 
     bool renderer_inited;
     bool renderer_reset;
+
+    /*
+     * virgl resources live inside virglrenderer, not on g->reslist, so we
+     * keep our own resource_id -> estimated host bytes map to be able to
+     * give the memory back on unref. Only used to enforce conf_max_hostmem.
+     */
+    GHashTable *virgl_hostmem;
+    bool virgl_hostmem_capped;
 };
 
 struct VhostUserGPU {
